<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

    <!--
        What the window is painted with - selection, hover, the panel and its edge, the rules, the
        scrim, the buttons, the focus ring. Brushes only, not one number and not one style.

        SPLIT OUT OF Colours.xaml ON 2026-09-16, and the seam was in that file's own first
        paragraph: it says it holds the colours that MEAN something about an entry, and eighteen of
        its twenty-nine brushes were furniture. It stood exactly on the markup ceiling of 376 the
        day a hover brush for the panel surfaces was needed, so the furniture moved here and the
        meanings stayed. The immediate cause is the one every split of a value file has had: a
        ceiling that may only go down, and a file standing on it.

        WHAT THAT SEAM MEANS FOR A READER: "what colour says a service is stopped" is answered in
        Colours.xaml, "what colour is the sheet under a plan" is answered here, and neither file
        has to be opened to know which to open.

        EVERY BRUSH HERE ANSWERS TO A FLOOR IN ContrastGuards, most of them floors this project
        chose rather than WCAG's, and each says beside itself what it is measured against. THE
        MEASUREMENT IS AGAINST THE WINDOW UNLESS THE COMMENT SAYS OTHERWISE - and since
        2026-09-16 one of them says otherwise, because a brush that clears its floor on the window
        can fail it on the panel: SurfaceHover reads 1.70 on the window and 1.30 on SurfacePanel.

        MERGED IMMEDIATELY AFTER Colours.xaml AND BEFORE EVERY STYLES FILE, like every value file.
        The styles reach these keys through StaticResource, resolved WHILE THE FILE IS BEING READ.
    -->

    <!--
        Selection, and it is here because the built-in theme does not cover it.

        Measured by looking: with ThemeMode="Dark" the Fluent theme styles the controls but
        leaves DataGrid's own selection alone, so a selected row came out light grey with
        dark text on a dark window - and the focused cell drew a box that reads as somewhere
        to type, on a list that is read only. That is the "two products in one window" this
        whole file exists to prevent, arriving from the one direction ADR-24 assumed was
        covered.
    -->
    <!--
        Selection has to be TELLABLE APART from an ordinary row, which is what WCAG 2.2
        SC 1.4.11 asks of a component state, so it answers to 3:1 against the list background
        and not to taste. The old #2D4F6C gave 1.90.

        Two ratios pull against each other here and both are checked: lighten the surface and
        it separates better from the background while white text on it separates worse.
        #3F7099 gives 3.09 against the background and 5.26 for white on top - the only pair in
        the search where both clear their threshold with room.

        Hover answers to a floor of OUR choosing and that is said plainly rather than dressed
        up as a standard: 1.4.11 covers states needed to IDENTIFY a component, and a pointer
        already says where the pointer is. What is not defensible is 1.14, which is what
        #2A2A2A gave - below the point where the state can be seen at all.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceSelected">#3F7099</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceHover">#454545</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        HOVER FOR EVERYTHING THAT STANDS ON THE PANEL SURFACE, 2026-09-16 - the list under the
        search box, the close mark and the copy buttons of the plan sheet. SurfaceHover above is
        measured against the window, where it reads 1.70, and every one of those controls stands
        on SurfacePanel (#343434), where the same brush reads 1.30 - under the 1.5 this project
        set as the point at which a state can be seen at all. So a row of the list under the box
        did not answer the pointer in any way a person could see, which is half of what the owner
        called unintuitive. Measured with tools/contrast/contrast.ps1 -Background '#343434'.

        #565656 reads 1.69 on the panel - the same distance SurfaceHover keeps from the window -
        and ContrastGuards measures it THERE, in the one test in that file that asks about the
        panel rather than the window.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceHoverOnPanel">#565656</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE ONE THING ON A SCREEN THAT MOST WANTS PRESSING - 2026-09-01 evening, owner's decision
        after a palette study, and the study is the reason this is one brush rather than two.

        WHAT WAS WRONG. Every control in the chrome - seventeen chips, seven action buttons, the
        column picker, the filter switch - was drawn from the same two greys, so a BUTTON meant
        nothing. Meanwhile SurfaceSelected did three jobs at once: the picked row, the chip that is
        on, and the button off the first screen. The one saturated colour in the window meant three
        things and the shape of a control meant none.

        THE OBVIOUS REPAIR WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED. A second, brighter blue for the primary button
        beside the selection blue: tools/contrast/contrast.ps1 gives #0F6CBD against #3F7099 a ratio
        of 1.02, and #0078D4 gives 1.16. Those are the same LIGHTNESS. They would differ in hue and
        in nothing else, and in nothing at all to somebody with reduced colour vision. So the
        families are told apart by SHAPE - pill, rectangle, rectangle - and colour is spent once.

        WHY THIS EXACT BLUE, AND THE SEARCH IS WHY IT IS NOT BRIGHTER. Two ratios pull against each
        other, the same pair SurfaceSelected was chosen by: lift the fill and it separates better
        from the window while white on it separates worse. Measured across the search - #1B72B8
        gives 3.22 against the window and 5.07 for white on top, and it is the only candidate that
        clears both thresholds with room. #1E7AC4 reads better against the window at 3.60 and drops
        white text to 4.53, which is the line rather than a margin. #2A7FC4 fails text outright at
        4.26.

        TWO CONTROLS WEAR IT AND THAT IS THE WHOLE LIST: the button that restarts this tool with
        administrator rights, which is the only way forward when the session has none, and the
        button that carries out a plan. Neither is ever on screen beside the other.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePrimaryAction">#1B72B8</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE SAME BUTTON UNDER THE POINTER AND UNDER THE FINGER, 2026-09-01, AND BOTH EXIST BECAUSE
        THE OWNER CALLED THE STATE THEY REPLACE UGLY. Hover was a WHITE border painted round the
        accent - TextOnSurface on the template's Face - so pointing at the one saturated control in
        the window put the brightest colour in the window round it, in a ring one point thick.

        WHY DARKER RATHER THAN LIGHTER, AND IT IS ARITHMETIC RATHER THAN TASTE. White on #1B72B8
        measures 5.07 and the text floor is 4.5, so the fill has almost no room to go UP: #1E7AC4
        already drops it to 4.53 and #2A7FC4 fails at 4.26 - the search beside the base colour has
        those numbers. Downwards there is no such wall, and every step makes the white text easier
        rather than harder: 6.06 hovering and 7.17 pressed.

        THE FLOOR EACH ANSWERS TO IS 1.5, THE SAME AS SurfaceHover, AND FOR THE SAME REASON WRITTEN
        THERE. SC 1.4.11 covers a state needed to IDENTIFY a component, and a shade saying where the
        pointer already is does not identify anything - the button was identified by the fill it has
        at rest, which clears 3:1 on its own. They measure 2.69 and 2.27 against the window.

        Both are the accent composited on the window at 85% and 72%, so they stay one family rather
        than three blues: a hue shift between rest and pressed is what SurfaceSelected was doing
        here before, and it read as a different control being pressed.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePrimaryActionHover">#1C66A1</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePrimaryActionPressed">#1C5B8D</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE ONLY THING IDENTIFYING A FILTER CHIP THAT IS OFF, from the same evening, and it answers
        to a higher ratio than any other edge in this file for exactly that reason.

        A chip at rest has no fill any more - that is what stops seventeen of them reading as the
        same control as the seven action buttons under them. What is left is the outline, so SC
        1.4.11 applies to it in full: 3:1 for what identifies a component. #6E6E6E measures 3.20.

        SurfacePanelEdge WAS TRIED FIRST AND IS NOT ENOUGH. It measures 1.84, which is right for a
        seam between two regions that are both already visible, and wrong for the one mark saying a
        control is there at all.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceChipEdge">#6E6E6E</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        The line between one row and the next, so a row can be carried across six columns and
        1650 pixels without the eye losing it. Complaint 2 of the eleven.

        IT IS A LINE AND NOT A ZEBRA STRIPE, AND THAT IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE.
        The owner asked for stripes on 2026-08-05. Three ways of drawing them were tried against
        the pixel and the first two failed:

          1. AlternationCount plus a trigger on ItemsControl.AlternationIndex - nothing painted.
             The grid coerces AlternationCount to nothing while AlternatingRowBackground is null.
          2. DataGrid.AlternatingRowBackground - painted, and DISQUALIFIED BY WHAT THAT PROVED.
             Set to one colour with our own row trigger set to another, the grid's colour is the
             one on screen. Left in, it would have taken selection, hover and the
             moved-a-moment-ago highlight off every second row, silently.
          3. A horizontal grid line - paints, at 1200,212 in the capture, and touches none of the
             three row states because the grid draws it rather than the row's background.

        WHY 2 BEAT OUR TRIGGER WAS WRITTEN HERE AS "COERCION" AND THAT WAS WRONG. Read out of
        their markup on 2026-08-05 with tools/wpfui-probe template: their row template carries
        a trigger on ItemsControl.AlternationIndex = 0 which sets the border's Background from
        the grid's RowBackground - and AlternationIndex is zero for every row while
        AlternationCount is, so that trigger is ALWAYS ON. A setter inside a control template's
        triggers outranks the TemplateBinding on the same element, so our row Background died
        there rather than to any coercion. Naming it coercion made the whole family look
        unfixable, when the real answer was to stop borrowing the template - see ListRow.

        1.25 IS OUR FLOOR AND NOT WCAG'S. SC 1.4.11 covers states needed to IDENTIFY a component -
        a separator identifies nothing, it only has to be seen between two rows. #333333 measures
        1.30 against the window, and deliberately stays under hover at 1.70 so that pointing at a
        row still reads as the stronger signal.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceRowLine">#333333</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE SCROLLBAR THUMB, ASLEEP AND AWAKE, 2026-09-02, AND THIS PAIR EXISTS BECAUSE ONE COLOUR
        COULD NOT DO BOTH JOBS. The bar was #6E6E6E at all times - 2.84 against the window, which
        made a strip of furniture the brightest thing on the list screen that is not text, and the
        owner's words were that it is too big and ugly.

        1.4 IS OURS AND IT IS THE LOWEST FLOOR IN THIS FILE ON PURPOSE. At rest this thumb answers
        one question - where am I in the list - for somebody who is not currently reaching for it.
        It identifies no component and it competes with nothing, so SC 1.4.11 does not reach it - it
        only has to stay ABOVE the line between two rows, which sits at 1.25 and carries less. The
        moment the pointer enters the bar the awake colour takes over at 3.79, which does clear
        1.4.11 - so the state somebody can actually act on is the state that answers to the standard.

        THE TWO ARE ALSO TELLABLE FROM EACH OTHER, which is the check a ratio against the window
        cannot make: 1.4 to 3.79 is a bigger step than any other pair of states in this theme.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceScrollThumb">#3A3A3A</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceScrollThumbAwake">#7A7A7A</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE PLAN PANEL'S OWN SURFACE, 2026-08-19, and it exists because the owner looked at the
        panel for the first time and the first thing he said was that it looks bad.

        HE WAS RIGHT AND THE CAUSE WAS THIS: the panel had no fill at all, so it was drawn
        straight over the list. The Account column ran under its first line and a selected row
        carried its blue behind the text. Nothing said the right hand side was a different
        surface, which is why the button at the bottom of it looked like it belonged to nothing.

        A LARGE REGION READS AT A LOWER RATIO THAN A THIN LINE, which is why this sits near the
        row line rather than near hover: it is a whole column of the window rather than a state
        a row takes, and lifting it further would make the list look like it was in a hole.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePanel">#343434</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        The line between the list and the panel. Stronger than the line between two rows, and
        deliberately: that one separates two things of the same kind, this one separates two
        regions doing different jobs, and it is the only edge the panel has.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfacePanelEdge">#4A4A4A</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        A row that moved a moment ago. A10 rule three: a change has to be visible rather than
        stealthy, because a list that quietly corrects itself is one where somebody looking
        away misses the thing they were waiting for.

        Deliberately dim. It marks every service that starts or stops while the window is
        open, which on a working machine is a steady trickle - loud enough to catch the eye
        from across the screen and it would be a fairground.

        DIM IS NOT THE SAME AS INVISIBLE, and #3A3320 was the second. It measured 1.30 against
        the background, and that number explains an argument this project could not settle for
        three slices: whether the colour was reaching a pixel at all. At 1.30 it hardly shows
        when it does arrive, so a rendering fault and a contrast fault looked identical from
        the outside. #63563A keeps the amber and measures 2.27.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceChanged">#63563A</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        WHERE THE KEYBOARD IS. The fourth row state, and the only one that is a formal
        requirement rather than a courtesy - WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.11 asks 3:1 of a focus indicator,
        and `ADR-24` removed the only one this list had when it took the box off the focused
        cell. That was the right call about appearance: the box read as somewhere to type, on a
        list nobody can type into. It was also the removal of the whole indicator.

        IT ANSWERS TO FOUR BACKGROUNDS, NOT ONE, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS THIS PALE. A ring is
        drawn against whatever the row already is, so the ratio that matters is the worst of
        the four a row can take. Measured with tools/contrast/contrast.ps1 against each:

          #202020  the window          11.21
          #454545  hover                6.60
          #63563A  moved a moment ago   4.94
          #3F7099  selected             3.62   <- the one that decided the colour

        Every brush above answers to the window background alone, which is the ordinary case
        and is what ContrastGuards measures. This one has its own test in that file, because a
        guard that checked it against the window would pass it at 11.21 and never look at the
        3.62 that is the real floor.

        DRAWN AS AN OUTLINE AND NOT A FILL, and that is not only taste. A fill would have to
        beat the three states it lands on top of, so focus and selection would fight over the
        same pixel. An outline sits at the edge and leaves the state underneath legible.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="FocusRing">#D6D6D6</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE DIMMING OVER THE WINDOW WHILE A PLAN IS ON SCREEN, 2026-09-02, AND IT IS THE ONE BRUSH
        HERE WHOSE JOB IS TO REMOVE CONTRAST RATHER THAN MAKE IT.

        Half-opaque black rather than a darker grey, because it works over whatever is behind it -
        the list, the first screen, the filters - and a flat fill would be wrong over two of three.

        IT HELPS THE NUMBERS RATHER THAN HURTING THEM: the sheet's #343434 sits at 1.25 against the
        bare window and better against a window reading #101010 under this. Measured on the capture,
        which also confirmed the arithmetic - red text under it came back at exactly half of every
        component. Its entry in ContrastGuards is the one floor asking for nothing.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceScrim">#80000000</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE INSET UNDER A COMMAND SOMEBODY IS ABOUT TO COPY, 2026-09-02. Darker than the sheet rather
        than lighter, which is what makes a block of text read as something to take away - a recess says "not prose".

        ITS RATIO IS MEASURED AGAINST THE WRONG THING - BACKLOG 286 IN ONE LINE. The table checks
        against the WINDOW, where this reads 1.08, and it is never drawn there: it sits inside the
        sheet, at 1.22. White on it is 16.7 either way.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceCommandBox">#262626</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE ONE BUTTON IN THIS PRODUCT THAT CHANGES A MACHINE, 2026-09-02. The owner asked for two
        red buttons and what went in is narrower on purpose: RED FILL MEANS "THIS CHANGES YOUR
        MACHINE" AND NOTHING ELSE. Restart as admin wears the same red as an OUTLINE instead, so it
        belongs to the red sentence it answers - two red fills is a window with no red left to spend.

        NOT DARKER, WHICH IS WHERE A RED BUTTON USUALLY ENDS UP, because white on it has to stay
        readable: 5.21 with white on top and 3.13 against the window, the same shape as the blue
        beside it at 5.07 and 3.22. THE FIRST TRY WAS #C0392B AND THE GUARD CAUGHT IT AT EXACTLY 3.00
        against a floor of 3.0 - my own arithmetic had rounded it up. Hover and pressed go darker.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceDestructiveAction">#C43C2E</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceDestructiveActionHover">#A32F23</SolidColorBrush>
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceDestructiveActionPressed">#8C271D</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        THE LINE UNDER THE COLUMN HEADINGS, 2026-09-25 - owner's complaint that the headings read as
        one more row. Stronger than the line between two rows (SurfaceRowLine, 1.29) because it
        separates the names of the columns from what is in them, which is a different kind of seam.
        1.84 against the window.

        ONE SURFACE FOR THE WHOLE WINDOW, AND THAT IS THE OWNER'S DECISION OF THE SAME DAY. Three
        drawings giving the commands, the list and the headings fills of their own were built and
        run, and he turned all three down as "many apps in one app". What sets things apart in this
        window is the things themselves - docs/PROJEKT-PALETA-20260925.md.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SurfaceHeadingRule">#4A4A4A</SolidColorBrush>

    <!--
        FOUR OF THE LIBRARY'S OWN KEYS, OVERRIDDEN BY NAME, 2026-09-25. WPF UI's button and toggle
        templates read these through DynamicResource (Button.xaml at tag 4.3.0, lines 24 and 83), so
        one line here reaches every button in the window without a fill of its own - a dozen of them.

        A DISABLED BUTTON LOSES ITS FILL. Measured: enabled #2D2D2D, disabled #2A2A2A - the only
        difference between a button that can be pressed and one that cannot was three steps of grey
        and the colour of the word. With no fill it keeps the library's faint outline and its dimmed
        word, and reads as a place where a button will be rather than as a button.

        AN ENABLED ONE GETS A LITTLE MORE. The library's fill is 6% white, 1.18 against the window.
        These are 10% at rest and 14% under the pointer, and they stay TRANSLUCENT on purpose: the
        same button stands on the window and on the panel, and a fraction of white lifts it off
        whichever it is on, where a solid grey chosen for the window vanishes on the lighter panel.
        Pressed stays the library's, which is darker than rest.

        THESE ARE WRITTEN AS ATTRIBUTES AND ContrastGuards DOES NOT SEE THEM - it reads six hex digits
        between tags. A known hole, and closing it is part of the two-layer palette in the same
        project document.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackground" Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPointerOver" Color="#24FFFFFF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackground" Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundPointerOver" Color="#24FFFFFF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ButtonBackgroundDisabled" Color="Transparent" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundDisabled" Color="Transparent" />

    <!--
        EVERY TEXT BOX, THROUGH THE LIBRARY'S OWN KEYS, 2026-09-25 - owner: the search box looks bad.
        Measured on the pixel: the box the window opens with the keyboard in was #1F1F1F on a #202020
        window - DARKER than what it stands on, a hole rather than a field - with a line under it
        three pixels thick in #686562. That line is WPF UI's focus line, and it takes Windows' own
        accent colour (TextControlFocusedBorderBrush = SystemAccentColorPrimary, Dark.xaml:612), so
        it wore whatever the machine's owner once chose for his taskbar.

        THE FIELD NOW STANDS OUT OF THE WINDOW RATHER THAN INTO IT: 7% white at rest AND with the
        keyboard in it, 10% under the pointer - a step under the buttons at 10 and 14, so a field and
        a button do not read as one control. Translucent for the reason the buttons are: the box of
        seconds stands on the plan sheet.

        THE FOCUS LINE IS THE SELECTION BLUE, the one the scope tabs are underlined with, so "this is
        where you are" is one colour in this window rather than one colour per machine.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackground" Color="#12FFFFFF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundFocused" Color="#12FFFFFF" />
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlBackgroundPointerOver" Color="#1AFFFFFF" />
    <!--
        A BRUSH OF ITS OWN, NOT AN ALIAS - and the alias was built first and failed on the owner's
        screen. <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlFocusedBorderBrush" ResourceKey="SurfaceSelected" />
        compiled and ran, and the library's template, which reads this key through DynamicResource,
        drew the focus line in its own grey (#454545 on the pixel, three device pixels thick - so the
        trigger fired and the colour did not arrive). The value is SurfaceSelected's, written twice
        until the two-layer palette gives both a colour to point at.
    -->
    <SolidColorBrush x:Key="TextControlFocusedBorderBrush" Color="#3F7099" />
</ResourceDictionary>
